  just ahead canadian researchers print up  a breakthrough in male infertility  in canada about one in six couples  struggle with infertility thirty percent  of the time the inability to conceive is  related to the male partner and often  it's not treatable but thanks to a team  of canadian researchers and clever use  of a 3d printer  some of those men are a step closer to  conceiving jamie morocco shows us how it  works  inside this vancouver lab the printers  aren't for paper  instead they're being used in hopes of  producing something unexpected sperm we  wanted to  really replicate what we see within the  natural human body and try to create  these tubules that are about the same  size as what we see in the actual  testicle so that gives us a pretty good  buffer in a world first dr ryan flanagan  and his team at the university of  british columbia 3d printed viable  testicular cells using stem cells they  obtained from a testicular biopsy from a  31 year old patient with non-obstructive  azospermia or noa where no sperm is  found in the ejaculate the team cultured  the cells when those continued to show a  positive viability rate they were 3d  printed onto a petri dish into a hollow  tube-like structure resembling the  sperm-producing tubules found inside the  testicles 12 days later the cells had  not only survived but thrived we got  partway to creating a full sperm so we  arrived at a middle stage of  that sperm production process for men  diagnosed with noa the current option is  surgery which can be invasive and  ineffective global experts say this  study is a game changer when you take  that hope away from patients where they  can't have kids and tell them there's no  option  and then you bring forward an option  such as what dr flanagan is proposing  that could make a huge difference  in the world of infertility flanagan is  motivated by the patients he hasn't been  able to help he says his team will now  try to coach the printed cells to  produce sperm facilitating cell to cell  interaction this is really the starting  point for for our research with the end  goal of giving more men the opportunity  to become biological fathers jamie  morocco global news toronto  coming up the story of the star wars kid  takes a dramatic turn  oh my god  this was in our bed  the entertainment world is looking back  at the life and legacy of new york  actress estelle harris who has died of  natural causes most famous for her  namesake role playing estelle george  costanz's mom on seinfeld harris began  her career quite late at the age of 50.  she also played mrs potato head in  pixar's toy story franchise and those  who worked with harris say she had one  of the best voices and best laughs of  anyone in the business estelle harris  was 93 years old  well if you were to write a book on the  history of the internet the chapter on  viral videos would have to include the  story of a quebec teen nineteen years  ago before social media or youtube a  video of jaslane reza exploded online  and he became known as the star wars kid  raza has rarely spoken about what  happened but he's breaking his silence  in a new national film board documentary  mike armstrong reports  it has somewhere in the neighborhood of  a billion views a number that's still  growing but it was in those first days  after shooting this video something no  one was ever supposed to see  that things exploded  within about two weeks it was on the  cover of the new york times gisleraza  was only 15 years old when classmates  found a video cassette he'd shot at  school  they uploaded his video to the web and  it snowballed  he blotted sounds and effects and it  kept growing  when producers from leno and letterman  and admittedly this journalist reached  out he and his parents turned down every  interview request  the fear was that people weren't  laughing with him but at him  at that time when you have a 15 year old  going through this  i'm not convinced more attention was the  the right remedy but the number one  viral video on the internet is the  so-called star wars kid  now 19 years later it has as part of a  documentary shining light on a story he  never before wanted told it's not just a  look at what happened but also what it  meant he revisits the trauma how he had  to leave school and how the media  attention forced his family to move out  of their home  i'm just like but he also meets one of  the people who originally spread the  video american blogger andy bayo was 25  years old when he saw it linked to it  and named it star wars kid  i have enormous regret  posting  the the video  i haven't talked to anybody about this  this is the first time and i am  i'm just profoundly sorry  now that's something he has uh never  heard from his former classmates an  apology his family sued the students and  settled out of court he's moved on he  studied law and rebuilt his life he's  never seen the other students again  now one sad irony in this story is how  his image was used and spread despite  being a minor the law would have  protected him better he says if instead  of making a video he committed a crime  mike armstrong global news montreal  and that is global national for this  sunday i'm chris galas  and tonight we want to leave you with  children leading a fundraising effort  for the ukrainian red cross  eleven-year-old carl and his friend  ten-year-old rowan from hundred mile  house bc launched a peace walk over  spring break with a modest 200 goal well  guess what they just hit 10 000  way to go kids  thanks very much for watching donna  friesen is back tomorrow night hope you  have a great night  [Music]
 

